.TH "WINDOW.SETINTERVAL" "3JS" "September 3, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Window.setInterval \- Window: setInterval() method
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Warning:\f[R] When the \f[CR]code\f[R] parameter is used, this
method dynamically executes its value as JavaScript.
APIs like this are known as injection sinks, and are potentially a
vector for cross\-site\-scripting (XSS) attacks.
.PP
You can mitigate this risk by always assigning \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R]
objects instead of strings and enforcing trusted types.
See Security considerations for more information.
.RE
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]setInterval()\f[B]\f[R] method of the \f[CR]Window\f[R]
interface repeatedly calls a function or executes a code snippet, with a
fixed time delay between each call.
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
setInterval(code)
setInterval(code, delay)

setInterval(func)
setInterval(func, delay)
setInterval(func, delay, param1)
setInterval(func, delay, param1, param2)
setInterval(func, delay, param1, param2, /* \&..., */ paramN)
.EE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]func\f[R]
A \f[CR]Function\f[R] to be executed every \f[CR]delay\f[R]
milliseconds.
The first execution happens after \f[CR]delay\f[R] milliseconds.
.TP
\f[B]code\f[R]
A \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R] or a string of arbitrary code that is
compiled and executed every \f[CR]delay\f[R] milliseconds.
This can be used instead of passing a function, but is \f[I]strongly
discouraged\f[R] for the same reasons that make using \f[CR]eval()\f[R]
a security risk.
.TP
\f[B]delay\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
The delay time between executions of the specified function or code, in
milliseconds.
Defaults to 0 if not specified.
See Delay restrictions below for details on the permitted range of
\f[CR]delay\f[R] values.
.TP
\f[B]param1\f[R], \&..., \f[B]paramN\f[R] \f[I](optional)\f[R]
Additional arguments which are passed through to the function specified
by \f[I]func\f[R] once the timer expires.
.SS Return value
A positive integer (typically within the range of 1 to 2,147,483,647)
that uniquely identifies the interval timer created by the call.
.PP
This identifier, often referred to as an \(lqinterval ID\(rq, can be
passed to \f[CR]clearInterval()\f[R] to stop the repeated execution of
the specified function.
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]SyntaxError\f[R]
The \f[CR]code\f[R] can\(cqt be parsed as a script.
.TP
\f[B]TypeError\f[R]
Thrown if the \f[CR]code\f[R] parameter is set to a string when Trusted
Types are enforced by a CSP and no default policy is defined.
It is also thrown if the first parameter is not one of the supported
types: a function, string or \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R].
.SH DESCRIPTION
The \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] function is commonly used to set a delay
for functions that are executed again and again, such as animations.
You can cancel the interval using \f[CR]clearInterval()\f[R].
.PP
If you wish to have your function called \f[I]once\f[R] after the
specified delay, use \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R].
.SS Delay restrictions
It\(cqs possible for intervals to be nested; that is, the callback for
\f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] can in turn call \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to
start another interval running, even though the first one is still
going.
To mitigate the potential impact this can have on performance, once
intervals are nested beyond five levels deep, the browser will
automatically enforce a 4 ms minimum value for the interval.
Attempts to specify a value less than 4 ms in deeply\-nested calls to
\f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] will be pinned to 4 ms.
.PP
Browsers may enforce even more stringent minimum values for the interval
under some circumstances, although these should not be common.
Note also that the actual amount of time that elapses between calls to
the callback may be longer than the given \f[CR]delay\f[R]; see Reasons
for delays longer than specified for examples.
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The \f[CR]delay\f[R] argument is converted to a signed
32\-bit integer, which limits the value to 2147483647 ms, or roughly
24.8 days.
.RE
.SS Interval IDs are shared with \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R]
The method returns an identifier that uniquely identifies the interval
timer created by the call.
This identifier, which is often referred to as an \(lqinterval ID\(rq,
can be passed to \f[CR]clearInterval()\f[R] to stop the repeated
execution of the specified function.
.PP
Within the same global environment (e.g., a particular window or
worker), the interval ID is ensured to remain unique and is not reused
for any new interval timer as long as the original timer is still
active.
However, different global environments maintain their own independent
pools of interval IDs.
.PP
Be aware that \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] and \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R] share
the same pool of IDs, and that \f[CR]clearInterval()\f[R] and
\f[CR]clearTimeout()\f[R] can technically be used interchangeably.
For clarity, however, you should try to always match them to avoid
confusion when maintaining your code.
.SS Ensure that execution duration is shorter than interval frequency
If there is a possibility that your logic could take longer to execute
than the interval time, it is recommended that you recursively call a
named function using \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R].
For example, if using \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to poll a remote server
every 5 seconds, network latency, an unresponsive server, and a host of
other issues could prevent the request from completing in its allotted
time.
As such, you may find yourself with queued up XHR requests that won\(cqt
necessarily return in order.
.PP
In these cases, a recursive \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R] pattern is
preferred:
.IP
.EX
(\f[B]function\f[R] loop() {
  setTimeout(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[I]// Your logic here\f[R]

    loop();
  }, delay);
})();
.EE
.PP
In the above snippet, a named function \f[CR]loop()\f[R] is declared and
is immediately executed.
\f[CR]loop()\f[R] is recursively called inside \f[CR]setTimeout()\f[R]
after the logic has completed executing.
While this pattern does not guarantee execution on a fixed interval, it
does guarantee that the previous interval has completed before
recursing.
.SS Functions are called with the global \f[CR]this\f[R]
The functions passed to \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] is run with normal
function call semantics for determining the reference of
\f[CR]this\f[R].
This problem is explained in detail in the JavaScript reference.
.PP
For non\-arrow functions, the \f[CR]this\f[R] context is set to the
\f[CR]globalThis\f[R] (an alias for \f[CR]window\f[R] in browsers)
object.
.PP
The following example demonstrates how this can cause unexpected
behavior.
Here, when we pass the method \f[CR]counter.count\f[R] directly to
\f[CR]setInterval()\f[R], the \f[CR]this\f[R] context is lost, and the
method is called on the global object instead of the \f[CR]Counter\f[R]
instance, resulting in a \f[CR]TypeError\f[R] when the \f[CR]count\f[R]
method tries to access \f[CR]this\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] Counter {
  constructor() {
    \f[B]this\f[R].data = \f[B]new\f[R] Map();
  }

  count(item) {
    \f[B]this\f[R].data.set(item, (\f[B]this\f[R].data.get(item) || 0) + 1);
  }
}

\f[B]const\f[R] counter = \f[B]new\f[R] Counter();

counter.count(\(dqfoo\(dq); \f[I]// Successfully adds \(dqfoo\(dq to the map\f[R]
setInterval(counter.count, 1000, \(dqbar\(dq);
\f[I]// TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading \(aqset\(aq)\f[R]
.EE
.PP
To work around this, you must make sure that the function passed to
\f[CR]setInterval\f[R] has the correct \f[CR]this\f[R] context.
There are three main ways to do this:
.IP "1." 3
If you want to explicitly specify the \f[CR]this\f[R] context, instead
of passing the method directly, wrap the method call in another
anonymous function that explicitly calls the method with the correct
context:
.RS 4
.IP
.EX
setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] counter.count(\(dqbar\(dq), 1000);
setInterval(\f[B]function\f[R] () {
  counter.count(\(dqbar\(dq);
}, 1000);
.EE
.RE
.IP "2." 3
If you want to use the \f[CR]this\f[R] context of the code that calls
\f[CR]setInterval()\f[R], always use an arrow function, which inherits
the \f[CR]this\f[R] context of its enclosing scope:
.RS 4
.PP
\f[B]Bad\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] Counter {
  \f[I]// \&...\f[R]
  repeatedCount(item) {
    \f[I]// BAD: the \(gathis\(ga context is lost in the callback\f[R]
    setInterval(\f[B]function\f[R] () {
      \f[B]this\f[R].data.set(item, (\f[B]this\f[R].data.get(item) || 0) + 1);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
.EE
.PP
\f[B]Good\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B]class\f[R] Counter {
  \f[I]// \&...\f[R]
  repeatedCount(item) {
    \f[I]// GOOD: the arrow function inherits the \(gathis\(ga context of \(garepeatedCount()\(ga\f[R]
    setInterval(() \f[B]=>\f[R] {
      \f[B]this\f[R].data.set(item, (\f[B]this\f[R].data.get(item) || 0) + 1);
    }, 1000);
  }
}
.EE
.RE
.IP "3." 3
If you want to avoid extra function wrappers (which increase memory
usage) while explicitly specifying the \f[CR]this\f[R] context, you can
use the \f[CR]Function.prototype.bind()\f[R] method to create a new
function with the correct \f[CR]this\f[R] context:
.RS 4
.IP
.EX
setInterval(counter.count.bind(counter), 1000, \(dqbar\(dq);
.EE
.RE
.SS Security considerations
The method can be used to execute arbitrary input passed in the
\f[CR]code\f[R] parameter.
If the input is a potentially unsafe string provided by a user, this is
a possible vector for Cross\-site\-scripting (XSS) attacks.
.PP
For example, the following code shows how \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] might
execute \f[CR]untrustedCode\f[R] provided by a user:
.PP
\f[B]Bad\f[R]
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] untrustedCode = \(dqalert(\(aqPotentially evil code!\(aq);\(dq;
\f[B]const\f[R] id = setInterval(untrustedCode, 1000);
.EE
.PP
Websites with a Content Security Policy (CSP) that specifies
\f[CR]script\-src\f[R] or \f[CR]default\-src\f[R] will prevent such code
running by default.
You can specify \f[CR]unsafe\-eval\f[R] in your CSP to allow
\f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to execute, but this is unsafe as it disables
one of the main protections of CSP.
.PP
See Inline JavaScript in the CSP guide.
.PP
If you must allow the scripts to run via \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] you
can mitigate these issues by always assigning \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R]
objects instead of strings, and enforcing trusted types using the
\f[CR]require\-trusted\-types\-for\f[R] CSP directive.
This ensures that the input is passed through a transformation function.
.PP
To allow \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R] to run, you will additionally need to
specify the \f[CR]trusted\-types\-eval\f[R] keyword in your CSP
\f[CR]script\-src\f[R] directive.
This acts in the same way as \f[CR]unsafe\-eval\f[R], but \f[I]only\f[R]
allows the method to evaluate if trusted types are enabled (if you were
to use \f[CR]unsafe\-eval\f[R] it would allow execution even on browsers
that do not support trusted types).
.PP
For example, the required CSP for your site might look like this:
.IP
.EX
Content\-Security\-Policy: require\-trusted\-types\-for \(aqscript\(aq; script\-src \(aq<your_allowlist>\(aq \(aqtrusted\-types\-eval\(aq
.EE
.PP
The behavior of the transformation function will depend on the specific
use case that requires a user\-provided script.
If possible you should lock the allowed scripts to exactly the code that
you trust to run.
If that is not possible, you might allow or block the use of certain
functions within the provided string.
.SH EXAMPLES
Note that these examples omit the use of trusted types for brevity.
See Using \f[CR]TrustedScript\f[R] in \f[CR]eval()\f[R] for code showing
the expected approach.
.SS Example 1: Basic syntax
The following example demonstrates \f[CR]setInterval()\f[R]\(cqs basic
syntax.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] intervalID = setInterval(myCallback, 500, \(dqParameter 1\(dq, \(dqParameter 2\(dq);

\f[B]function\f[R] myCallback(a, b) {
  \f[I]// Your code here\f[R]
  \f[I]// Parameters are purely optional.\f[R]
  console.log(a);
  console.log(b);
}
.EE
.SS Example 2: Alternating two colors
The following example calls the \f[CR]flashtext()\f[R] function once a
second until the Stop button is pressed.
.SS HTML
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] id=\(dqmy_box\(dq>
  <\f[B]h3\f[R]>Hello World</\f[B]h3\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqstart\(dq>Start</\f[B]button\f[R]>
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqstop\(dq>Stop</\f[B]button\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
.IP
.EX
\&.go {
  \f[B]color\f[R]: green;
}
\&.stop {
  \f[B]color\f[R]: red;
}
.EE
.SS JavaScript
.IP
.EX
\f[I]// variable to store our intervalID\f[R]
\f[B]let\f[R] intervalId;

\f[B]function\f[R] changeColor() {
  \f[I]// check if an interval has already been set up\f[R]
  intervalId ??= setInterval(flashText, 1000);
}

\f[B]function\f[R] flashText() {
  \f[B]const\f[R] oElem = document.getElementById(\(dqmy_box\(dq);
  oElem.className = oElem.className === \(dqgo\(dq ? \(dqstop\(dq : \(dqgo\(dq;
}

\f[B]function\f[R] stopTextColor() {
  clearInterval(intervalId);
  \f[I]// release our intervalId from the variable\f[R]
  intervalId = \f[B]null\f[R];
}

document.getElementById(\(dqstart\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, changeColor);
document.getElementById(\(dqstop\(dq).addEventListener(\(dqclick\(dq, stopTextColor);
.EE
